# Line endings, and why this file is load-bearing
# ------------------------------------------------
# This project pins files by SHA-256. fta_web/core/BASELINE.json records a hash
# for every frozen file in desktop/ (src/, tests/, data/) and for every vendored file in
# fta_web/core/, and fta_web/tests/test_vendor_integrity.py fails if any of them
# moves. That is a contract about *bytes*.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A CRLF copy of a file is a different sequence of bytes, so it has a
# different SHA-256 -- and every single pin breaks at once, on a tree where
# nobody has edited anything.
#
# This is not hypothetical and it is not only about CI. It was found by the
# Windows job in .github/workflows/tests.yml on its first ever run: 12 failures,
# every hash-pinned file including data nobody had touched. Each reported hash
# was confirmed to be exactly the CRLF conversion of the LF original.
#
# It matters off CI too:
#   - A contributor cloning on Windows would see a dozen failures on a clean
#     checkout and reasonably conclude the repo was broken.
#   - The PyInstaller bundle ships fta_web/core/ as source specifically so an
#     auditor can hash it against BASELINE.json (see build/fta_editor.spec).
#     On Windows that audit could never pass.
#
# Normalize to LF in the working tree on every platform. Nothing here is a
# Windows shell script (.bat/.cmd/.ps1), which is the usual reason to want CRLF
# checked out; if one is ever added, give it an explicit `eol=crlf` line below
# -- and keep it out of the pinned directories.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The hash-pinned trees, stated again and more strongly.
#
# `-text` means "never do end-of-line conversion on these files, in either
# direction, whatever the platform or the user's core.autocrlf". The rule above
# would already cover them, but these are the files where a silent byte change
# breaks the integrity guarantee rather than merely causing a noisy diff, so
# they do not rely on `text=auto` guessing right.
desktop/**          -text
fta_web/core/**     -text
fta_web/examples/** -text

# Vendored third-party build, checked in verbatim. Not hash-pinned, but there is
# no reason to let Git rewrite a 1.1 MB generated artifact either.
fta_web/static/vendor/** -text

# Keep diffs of the lockfile readable but never let a tool reformat it.
uv.lock text eol=lf
